title: SMART: Error Counter Monitoring
agents: linux
catalog: os/storage
license: GPL
distribution: check_mk
description:
 This check monitors SMART error counters on storage devices that support
 SCSI error counter logs. It tracks detailed error statistics including
 corrected and uncorrected errors across read, write, and verify operations.

 The check reports individual error types only when they occur (> 0), 
 providing clean and focused monitoring output. Each error type is 
 evaluated against configurable thresholds with separate states.

 This check requires the {smartmontools} package to be installed on the
 monitored host. The agent plugin uses {smartctl} to collect error counter
 data from storage devices.

 The plugin discovers devices that support SCSI error counter logs, which
 are SAS and SCSI drives. SATA, NVMe and USB drives have no such log and are
 not monitored.

 By default the agent plugin does not spin up a disk in STANDBY or SLEEP
 mode. The service of such a disk is OK with "Disk in standby, not checked".
 The agent bakery rule "OPOSS SMART Error Monitoring (smartctl)" can wake
 these disks at every run or after a number of skipped runs.

 Each operation type (read, write, verify) generates detailed metrics:
 errors corrected by ECC fast/delayed, errors corrected by rereads/rewrites,
 correction algorithm invocations, bytes processed, and total uncorrected errors.
 
 The service output shows individual error types (for example
 "Read ECC fast: 5") with their own states based on configured thresholds,
 plus data processed per operation type.

 For SAS drives the check also shows the number of entries in the grown
 defect list, the sectors the drive has remapped since it left the factory.
 It is graphed, and gets a state only when a threshold rule is set.

item:
 The device path followed by the last 8 characters of the serial number in
 brackets, for example {/dev/sda (2JG5LEHB)}. A disk behind a RAID controller
 also carries its smartctl device type: {/dev/bus/0 megaraid,1 (2JG5LEHB)}.

discovery:
 One service is created for each storage device that supports SCSI error
 counter logs and has meaningful error counter data available. Services
 are named "SMART Errors <item>" (for example "SMART Errors /dev/sda (2JG5LEHB)").
 A SAS drive that reports its serial number but no error counter data is
 broken; it also gets a service, which is CRIT and shows the reason smartctl
 gives, for example "device Test Unit Ready [medium or hardware error (serious)]".
 A disk in standby keeps its service when the agent plugin has read it once;
 a disk that has slept since the plugin was installed is found once it is awake.
